      "solution": "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nint main() {\n    char A[35], B[35];\n    scanf(\"%s\", A);\n    scanf(\"%s\", B);\n\n    int n = strlen(A);\n    int carry = 0;\n    char result[40];\n    int index = 0;\n\n    for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n        int a = A[i] - '0';\n        int b = B[i] - '0';\n\n        int sum = a ^ b ^ carry;\n        carry = (a & b) | (carry & (a ^ b));\n\n        result[index++] = sum + '0';\n    }\n\n    if (carry) {\n        result[index++] = '1';\n    }\n\n    for (int i = index - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n        printf(\"%c\", result[i]);\n    }\n\n    printf(\"\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}",
      "statement": "This is <<Chapter 3>> of the <<Digital Logic Basics>> series.\n\nIn this chapter, you will simulate binary addition using logic gates.\n\nA binary adder takes two bits and produces:\n- a sum bit\n- a carry bit\n\nYou will evaluate a multi-bit addition using chained adders.\n\nEach bit position uses a FULL ADDER, defined as:\n\nsum = A XOR B XOR Cin\ncarry = (A AND B) OR (Cin AND (A XOR B))\n\nWhere:\n- A and B are the input bits\n- Cin is the carry-in from the previous position\n\nThe least significant bit (rightmost) has Cin = 0.\n\nGiven two binary numbers of equal length, you must compute:\n- the final binary sum\n- including the final carry (if any)",
